-> start

=== start ===

#title: The Signal

The tide rose before its time and covered the stone steps. High in the tower, a thin light cuts through the fog.

Across the bay, someone lights a lantern and answers.

You have lived in this village for eleven years, and in all that time the sea has never once come this far. The fishermen say it is the moon. The old women say it is a debt, and that the water is only collecting what it is owed.

The pier is gone. Where the boats were tied this morning there is only a line of black posts, standing out of the water like the teeth of something that has just surrendered a meal. A gull sits on the nearest one and does not move, not even when the first cold wave slaps against the wall below you.

Behind you, the village is dark. Nobody has lit a window. Nobody has opened a door. It is as if everyone had agreed, without a word, to let you be the one who stands here and watches.

The fog thickens, folds in on itself, and for a moment the light in the tower disappears. Then it returns, steady and patient, answering the lantern across the bay as if the two of them had been talking for years and you had only now learned to listen.

+ [Climb to the lantern] -> tower
+ [Go back to the beach] -> beach

=== tower ===
#title: The Lantern

The stairs creak with every step. Up top, the wind carries the smell of salt and burnt iron.

There are one hundred and forty steps, and you count every one of them because counting is easier than thinking. The walls are damp, and somewhere below, a door that you were sure you had closed swings slowly open and bangs once against the stone.

On the landing halfway up, there is a small window with no glass. Through it you can see the whole bay: the black water, the white line of surf, the faint orange point of the other lantern on the far shore. It has not moved since you started climbing. It is waiting for you.

At the top, the keeper's room is exactly as it must have been the day he left. A cup of tea, long cold, still sits on the table beside an open logbook. The last entry is written in a careful, shaking hand: "The water has come to the steps. I have answered it. God forgive me, I have answered it."

The lamp itself stands in the middle of the room, huge and quiet, a cage of brass and glass around a wick no thicker than your finger. It is not lit. And yet the light you saw from the beach, the one that cut through the fog, is coming from somewhere in this room, and you cannot find where.

+ [Light the lamp] -> ending
+ [Put out the lantern] -> ending

=== beach ===
#title: The Beach

The sand holds the marks of a walk that is not yours.

The footprints are deep and wet, and each one is slowly filling with seawater. They come out of the surf, cross the beach in a perfectly straight line, and stop at the foot of the tower steps. There they turn around, and go back into the sea.

Whoever made them was barefoot, and walked like someone who knew exactly where they were going and had been there many times before. The stride is long. The toes are spread wide, wider than any toes you have ever seen on a living person.

You crouch beside the nearest print and put your hand into it. The water is warm, warmer than the sea, warmer than the air. It smells faintly of smoke. When you lift your hand, a thin thread of light clings to your fingers for a moment before it fades.

+ [Follow the footprints] -> ending

=== ending ===
The lighthouse blinks once, then again.

Far across the bay, the other lantern answers, and you understand at last that it was never a signal for anyone else. It was a conversation, and you have just been invited to join it.

The tide turns. The black posts of the pier sink slowly back into the water, one by one, until there is nothing left to show that the sea ever came this far. In the morning the village will wake, and the fishermen will say it was the moon.

-> END
